Follows the citation round, which returned two results that change what the
measurements mean.

**Lewin verification: GIS-is-a-torsor CONFIRMED-BY-SOURCE**, and it names the
composition-order fact my harness had already measured. So P2 now carries an
INDEPENDENT cross-check of the reversed-order count instead of just reporting
it: the reversed order passes axiom A on exactly the triples whose two
intervals COMMUTE, and the class equation puts the number of commuting ordered
pairs at |G|·k for k conjugacy classes. Measured k = 9, so 24·9 = 216
commuting pairs (measured 216) and 24·216 = 5184 (measured 5184). Two routes,
one integer — the harness is measuring commutativity, which is what it claims.

**Mazzola verification: "clef as a chart" REFUTED from his own text** — clefs
are *prima vista Predicates*, a different semiotic stratum from denotators,
and his atlas covering is CONSTITUTIVE of the object (no refinement admitted),
which a re-choosable notation convention is not. The constructive correction
his sources supply is **Syn** (synonymy) / address change — his worked instance
being the fifth-circle automorphism of ℤ₁₂.

P6 measures what that correction implies, and the answer sharpens the user's
hypothesis rather than confirming it flat:

* the ORIGIN freedom (clef) is a FREE action at all 10 moduli — 0 fixed
  points everywhere. It IS a torsor. "A clef is an origin choice, not data"
  survives.
* the RENAMING freedom (Syn / automorphism) is NOT free at ANY modulus —
  every non-identity automorphism fixes the identity element. Renaming
  DISTINGUISHES a point.
* the full chart-freedom group is the holomorph ℤ/n ⋊ Aut(ℤ/n): transitive
  but not free (multiplicity 4 uniformly at n=12), so **not** a torsor. The
  torsor is exactly its normal translation subgroup. Conflating the two
  freedoms would have made the torsor claim false.
* n=12 is not distinguished: φ(12)=4, ranking LAST of the 10 moduli swept.
  Every prime modulus beats it. Whatever makes 12 prevalent, the interval
  algebra cannot see it — which is what a frame-free carrier should report.

